.Im MicroEmacs modes
.so me.tni
.TH cursor 2m
.XI cursor - "Buffer's cursor visibility"
.SH NAME
cursor \- Buffer's cursor visibility
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B "cursor Mode"
.sp
\fBC\fR \- mode line letter.
.SH DESCRIPTION
When  enabled  the text  cursor  is drawn  while the  buffer is current,  when
disabled  the cursor  is hidden.  \fBcursor\fR  is enabled by default.  As the
mode is a property of the buffer,  the cursor  re-appears  as soon as  another
buffer  is made  current;  there is no need to restore the mode on leaving the
buffer.
.PP
The mode is  typically  disabled by macros  which draw their own  display, see
.Ht Metris 3
and
.Ht Patience 3 ,
where a flashing text cursor is a distraction.
.PP
The cursor  is always  drawn when  \*(mS  is  prompting  for input on the
message line,  regardless of the current buffer's  \fBcursor\fR  mode, so that
the user can see what they are typing.
.PP
In a buffer created by
.Ht ipipe-shell-command 2
the mode is set and cleared by the child process using the terminal  sequences
\fBESC [ ? 25 h\fR and \fBESC [ ? 25 l\fR  respectively.  A child which  exits
while the cursor is hidden leaves the mode disabled,  exactly as it would leave
a real terminal.
.SH NOTES
Cursor hiding is not supported on all platforms and does nothing in \*(nE.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.na
